<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>


<worksheet xml:id="ws-divisibility-activity">

	<title>Module 2: Divisibility of Integers, Division Algorithm, Integer Congruence</title>
	<introduction><p><alert>The Leftovers</alert></p></introduction>
	
	<page>

	<paragraphs>
		  <title>The Integers</title>

 	 <p>
 	   We'll assume the following about the integers as <em>axioms</em>.
  	  We don't prove axioms, we use them as building blocks to prove them.
  	  They give us a floor to stand on.
  	  Otherwise it's turtles all the way down.
  	</p>

	<axiom>
		<title>Axioms of Integer Arithmetic</title>
		
		<statement>
		<p>
		<ul>
			<li><em>The integers are closed under addition and multiplication</em>, meaning that for all integers <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>, both <m>a+b</m> and <m>ab</m> are also integers.</li>
			<li><em>Addition and multiplication are commutative</em>, meaning that for all integers <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>, <m>a+b = b+a</m> and <m>ab = ba</m>.</li>
			<li><em>Addition and multiplication are associative</em>, meaning that for all integers <m>a, b</m> and <m>c</m>, <m>(a+b)+c = a+(b+c)</m> and <m>(ab)c = a(bc).</m></li>
			<li><em>Multiplication distributes over addition</em>, meaning that <m>a(b+c) = ab+ac</m> for all integers <m>a, b,</m> and <m>c</m>.</li>
			<li><em>The integer 0 is an additive identity</em>, meaning that <m>a+0 = a</m> for every integer <m>a</m>.</li>
			<li><em>The integer 1 is a multiplicative identity</em>, meaning that <m>1a = a</m> for every integer <m>a</m>.</li>
			<li><em>Every integer <m>a</m> has an additive inverse</em>, typically denoted <m>-a</m>; in particular, <m>a + (-a) = 0</m> for every integer <m>a</m>.</li>
		</ul>
		</p>
		</statement>
	</axiom>
 	 <!-- <image source="Module2-IntegerAxioms"/> -->
 	 <p>
   	 	Which axioms of a field are the integers missing?
  	</p>
    </paragraphs>
    </page>


	<page>
	
	<paragraphs>
		<title>Divides</title>

  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        An integer <m>a</m> <term>divides</term> an integer <m>b</m>, denoted
        <m>a\mid b</m>, if there exists an integer <m>q</m> such that
        <m>b=aq</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <p>
    In a popular high school mathematics textbook,
    students are told that one of the theorems in number theory is the following:
  </p>

  <theorem xml:id="divides-div-thm">
    <p>
      If <m>a,b,</m> and <m>c</m> are integers where <m>a</m> is a factor of
      <m>b</m> and <m>a</m> is a factor of <m>c</m>, then <m>a</m> is a factor
      of <m>b+c</m>.
    </p>
  </theorem>

  <exercise workspace="3in">
  	<statement>
        <p>
          Prove <xref ref="divides-div-thm" />.
          It may help to translate this to use the word <q>divides</q>.
        </p>
      </statement>
   </exercise>

   <exercise workspace="3in">
   	<statement>
        <p>
          Students are then asked about generalizing the theorem to:
          <theorem>
            <p>
              If <m>a,b,</m> and <m>c</m> are integers where <m>a</m> is a
              factor of <m>b</m> and <m>a</m> is a factor of <m>c</m>, then
              <m>a</m> is a factor of <m>bm+cn</m> for all integers <m>m</m> and
              <m>n</m>.
            </p>
          </theorem>
          Is this true?
          Prove or disprove.
        </p>
      </statement>
     </exercise>
    
    </paragraphs>

	</page>
	
	
	
	
	<page>
	
	<paragraphs>
		
		 <title>The Division Algorithm</title>

		<theorem>
			<title>The Division Algorithm</title>
			<statement>
				<p>Let <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> be integers with <m>a&gt;0</m>.
				Then there exist unique integers <m>q</m> and <m>r</m> such that</p>
					<md>b = aq + r \text{ and } 0\leq r &lt;a</md>
 				<p>
   					 We call <m>q</m> the <term>quotient</term> and <m>r</m> the <term>remainder</term>.
  				</p>
			</statement>
		</theorem>

		<exercise workspace="1in">
        <statement>
        <p>
          Practice one more time: Come up with two integers <m>a</m> and
          <m>b</m> that satisfy the <em>hypothesis</em> of the division
          algorithm.
          (If you want a challenge, throw in a negative integer.) Trade these
          numbers with another group member and then apply the division
          algorithm to the numbers you receive.
        </p>
		</statement>
		</exercise>
 		
		<exercise workspace="1in">
		<statement>
        <p>
          What is different about applying the division algorithm when
          <m>b&lt;0</m>?
        </p>
     	</statement>
		</exercise>

     	<exercise workspace="1in">
		<statement>
        <p>
          Do you think we should we assume the division algorithm as an axiom or
          try to prove it?
        </p>
     	</statement>
		</exercise>

		<exercise workspace="2in">
		<statement>
		<p>
          Spoiler alert: We will prove it, since it turns out we can derive it
          from another, simpler axiom.
          To start, let's do an example.
          Let <m>a = 10</m> and <m>b=-58</m>.
          Consider
          <md>
            S = \{ x\in \mathbb{Z}: x\ge 0 \text{ and }x=b-am \text{ for some }m\in\mathbb{Z}\} .
          </md>
          Find some elements of <m>S</m> and what appears to be the least
          element of <m>S</m>?
        </p>
		</statement>
		</exercise>
    
    </paragraphs>

	</page>
	
	
	<page>
	<paragraphs>
	<title>More Division Algorithm</title>
	
	<exercise workspace="1in">
	<statement>
        <p>
          If <m>b\ge 0</m> prove that <m>b\in S</m>.
          (Use the definition of the set <m>S</m>.) If <m>b&lt;0</m>, is
          <m>b\in S</m>?
          <!-- <fn><p>If <m>b&lt;0</m> then <m>b\notin S</m>.</p></fn> -->
        </p>
     </statement>
     </exercise>

     <exercise workspace="1in">
     <statement>
        <p>
          We will take the following as an axiom:
        </p>

        <axiom>
        	<title>The Well-Ordering Principle</title>
			<statement><p>Every nonempty subset of the whole numbers contains a least element.</p></statement>
		</axiom>

        <p>
          Do you think <m>S</m> as defined earlier is always a nonempty subset
          of the whole numbers?
          What element can you be sure is in <m>S</m> if <m>b&lt;0</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint><p>Choose <m>m=b</m> and show that <m>x=b-am\ge 0</m> and so <m>x\in S</m>.</p></hint>
      </exercise>

      <exercise workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          At this point you are hopefully thinking that <m>S</m> is a nonempty
          subset of the whole numbers.
          So, we can apply the well-ordering principle and get a least element.
          We will show the least element is the remainder.
          So, let <m>r</m> be the least element of <m>S</m>.
          Since <m>r\in S</m>, we know there is an integer <m>q</m> such that
          <m>r = b-aq</m>.
          Thus <m>b = aq + r</m>.
          That's good news!
          We also need that <m>0\le r &lt; a</m>.
          Why do we know <m>0\le r</m>?
        </p>
	   </statement>
	   </exercise>
	   
 		<exercise workspace="1in">
		<statement>
        <p>
          Now let's show <m>r&lt;a</m>.
          Suppose, for the sake of a contradiction that <m>r\ge a</m>.
          Show <m>r-a\in S</m>.
          (Recall <m>r\in S</m> and <m>r=b-aq</m>.)
        </p>
      	</statement>
		</exercise>

      	<exercise>
          <statement><p>You've reached a contradiction, how?</p></statement>
        </exercise>

		<exercise>
		<statement>
        <p>
          What remains to be proven to show the division algorithm is that
          <m>q</m> and <m>r</m> is unique.
          You can find a partial proof near the end of Investigation 1, which will help you on your first problem set!
        </p>
		</statement>
		</exercise>
	
	</paragraphs>
	</page>
	
	
	<page>
	<paragraphs>
		
		  <title>Congruence</title>

  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>n</m> be a natural number, and let <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> be
        integers.
        Then <m>a</m> <term>is congruent to <m>b</m> modulo <m>n</m></term>
        denoted <m>a\equiv b \pmod{n}</m> provided that <m>n</m> divides
        <m>a-b</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  
  <p><em>Examples:</em> Here's a chance to remember congruences:</p>
  <exercise workspace="1in">
  <statement>
      <p>
        Give an example of two integers that are congruent modulo <m>8</m>,
        where one is positive and one is negative.
      </p>
	</statement>
	</exercise>

   <exercise workspace="1in">
  <statement>
      <p>
        Characterize all the integers <m>m</m> such that
        <m>m\equiv 1 \pmod{3}</m>.
        What are all the integers <m>m</m> such that <m>m\equiv 4 \pmod{3}</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    </exercise>

   <exercise workspace="2in">
  <statement>
      <p>
        Prove that, for all <m>a,b,c,d\in\mathbb{Z}</m> and
        <m>n\in \mathbb{N}</m>, if <m>a\equiv b \pmod{n}</m> and
        <m>c\equiv d \pmod{n}</m> then <m>(a+c) \equiv (b+d) \pmod{n}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="2in">
  <statement>
      <p>
        Prove that for all <m>a,b,c,d\in\mathbb{Z}</m> and
        <m>n\in\mathbb{N}</m>, if <m>a\equiv b \pmod{n}</m> and
        <m>c\equiv d \pmod{n}</m> then <m>ac \equiv bd \pmod{n}</m>.
      </p>
	</statement>
	</exercise>


    </paragraphs>

	</page>
	
	
	<page>
	<paragraphs>
	<title>Fun with Congruence (and a connection to fields)</title>

	<exercise workspace="2in">
    <statement>
        <p>
          Consider the set <m>A = \{0,1,2,3,\dots, 7\}</m> and define addition
          by <m>x\oplus_{8} y = (x+y) \pmod{8}</m> and multiplication by
          <m>x\otimes_{8} y = xy \pmod{8}</m>.
          Find the multiplicative and additive identities of
          <m>(A, \oplus, \otimes)</m> (if they exist), and find the additive and
          multiplicative inverses of each element (if they exist).
          Will this be a field?
        </p>
       </statement>
       </exercise>

	<exercise>
    <statement>
        <p>
          <em>Check digits:</em> A UPC is a Universal Product Code, or the
          barcode on products.
          UPCs consist of 12 digits, and the last digit is known as a check
          digit.
          A check digit is there for error detection when a code is entered
          (they do this with your routing numbers for banks too).
          If the first 11 digits are <m>x_{1}, x_{2},\dots, x_{11})</m>, the
          check digit will be <m>x_{12}</m>
          <md>
            x_{12}\equiv -(x_{1}, x_{2}, \dots, x_{11}) \cdot (3,1,3,1,3,1,3,1,3,1,3) \pmod{10}
          </md>
          where <m>\cdot</m> is the dot product, so multiply the entries in the
          same place, and then add up all the results.
          Verify that the check digit is correct on the box of Famous Amos
          cookies.
          Do the multiplication in your head using modular arithmetic!
        </p>

        <sidebyside widths="30% 70%"><image source="images/Divides-UPC.png" />
        
        <p></p>
        </sidebyside>
        
	</statement>
	</exercise>
	

	<exercise workspace="2in">
    <statement>
        <p>
          My birthday was on a Thursday this year.
          Determine the next time my birthday will be on a Thursday again.
          (Use modular arithmetic!
          Also, leap years happen every 4 years, in years congruent to <m>0</m>
          mod <m>4</m>.) When will my birthday next be on a Sunday?
        </p>
	</statement>
	</exercise>
	   
    </paragraphs>

	</page>
	
<page>
<paragraphs>
<title>Review questions</title>

  <p>Make sure you can answer the following questions:</p>

<ul>
	<li><p>
        What is the definition of <q><m>a</m> divides <m>b</m></q>?
        Does <m>0 \mid x</m> where <m>x\in \mathbb{Z}</m>?
        Does <m>x\mid 0</m> where <m>x\in \mathbb{Z}</m>.
      </p></li>
                
<li><p>
        State the division algorithm and apply to any pair of integers
        satisfying the hypothesis.
      </p></li>
      
<li><p>
		State the definition of <q><m>a\equiv b\pmod{n}</m></q> and be able to
        determine if any pair of integers <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> are congruent
        modulo <m>n</m>.
		</p></li>
</ul>
                
<p>The rest of this page is for you to summarize important ideas from the module.</p>

</paragraphs>

</page>


<page>

<paragraphs>
    <title>Weekly Practice 2</title>

		<exercise number="1">
		<title>Fun (?) with fields (?)</title>
		<statement><p>
          Consider the set <m>\{0,1,2,3,4,5,6\}</m> and define addition by
          <m>x\oplus_{7} y = (x+y) \pmod{7}</m> and multiplication by
          <m>x\otimes_{7} y = xy \pmod{7}</m>.
          Find the multiplicative and additive identities of this number system
          (if they exist), and find the additive and multiplicative inverses of
          each element (if they exist).
          Is this set a field?
        </p></statement>
    	</exercise>
	
		<exercise number="2">
		<title>Another way to talk about congruences</title>
		<introduction><p>Consider the following theorem:</p>
		<theorem>
            <statement>
              <p>
                Let <m>n</m> be a natural number, and let <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>
                be integers.
                Then <m>a\equiv b \pmod{n}</m> if and only if <m>a</m> and
                <m>b</m> yield the same remainder when divided by <m>n</m>.
              </p>
            </statement>
          </theorem>
		</introduction>
        	<task><p>Do a couple examples of the theorem (in both directions!) below
                to gain a better understanding of what the theorem says.</p></task>
            <task><p>Prove (at least) one direction of the theorem.</p></task>
		</exercise>
		
		
		<exercise number="3">
		<title>UPCs!</title>
		<statement><p>Find a product with a UPC and verify the check digit.
		</p></statement>
		
		</exercise>
		
		<exercise number="4">
		<title>Another way congruences are weird</title>
		<introduction><p>In high school algebra, linear equations, like <m>5x+3 = 7</m> always
          had one solution.
          Consider the set <m>A = \{0,1,2,3\}</m> and define addition by
          <m>x\oplus_{4} y = (x+y) \pmod{4}</m> and multiplication by
          <m>x\otimes_{4} y = xy \pmod{4}</m>.
		</p></introduction>
			<task><p>
                Show that in <m>(A,\oplus_{4}, \otimes_{4})</m> the equation
                <m>2\otimes_{4} x \oplus_{4} 1 = 0</m> has no solutions.
              </p></task>
			
			<task><p>
                Find a linear equation in the number system
                <m>(A,\oplus_{4}, \otimes_{4})</m> that has 2 solutions.
              </p></task>
		</exercise>
	
	</paragraphs>
	
 </page>
 
 </worksheet>
